  {
    "label": "ac9b5d16-e129-5bbc-9ccb-344cad0daab4",
    "record": {
      "question": {
        ":question/id": "ac9b5d16-e129-5bbc-9ccb-344cad0daab4",
        ":question/problem": "![](images/84/849535b983a64c97b4cf06afcc7da7598349dc3f14f771cd3ae20db2c0742e8b.png)\n\nWhich of the following could be the equation of the polynomial curve shown above?",
        ":question/worked-solution": "First, we notice that the function has precisely three distinct roots, $x=-2,$ $x=0$ and $x=3.$\n\nBased on the graph, we can make the following conclusions about the multiplicities of the roots:\n\n- Since the graph is *not* tangent to the $x$-axis at $x=-2,$ this root is a simple root (the multiplicity equals $1$).\n\n- Since the graph passes through $x=0,$ this root has odd multiplicity, and since the graph is tangent to the $x$-axis at $x=0,$ the multiplicity must be greater than one.\n\n- Since the graph is *not* tangent to the $x$-axis at $x=3,$ this root is a simple root (the multiplicity equals $1$).\n\nTherefore, the only possible equation is $y=\\frac{1}{5}{x}^{3}(x+2)(x-3).$",
        ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/moderate",
        ":question/answer-fields": [
          {
            ":answer-field/id": "aef2415b-6234-5ebf-b592-60e406e41efb",
            ":answer-field/key": "selection",
            ":answer-field/type": ":answer-field.type/radio",
